<!--
Two optional frontmatter fields carry this task's relationships. Both are lists, both
are checked by `scripts/check-task-links.mjs` in pre-commit, and both are omitted when
they have nothing to say — an empty field and an absent one mean the same thing.

  resolves: [P003]      the pendings in PENDINGS.md this task answers (today only
                        pendings; the model also foresees lessons, once that ledger exists)
  blocked_by: [T012]    the tasks that have to finish before this one can start. Never
                        for a file another task also changes: task-units.mjs --plan
                        settles that before each unit is built, by a single writer too

Why fields and not a sentence in the body: a relationship written in prose is
invisible the day one side closes. With the field, closing this task while P003 is still
open is an error the hook reports, and a block that lifts (T012 reaching `done`) is
reported as a notice by that same hook. Neither closure is ever propagated automatically —
that would guess your intent. You close the other side, or you remove the field.

One limit worth knowing, stated rather than glossed: the notices (a block lifted, a
blocker dropped, a pending left without its candidate) are printed by the pre-commit hook,
which on a normal commit scrolls past. Nothing surfaces them when you open a session yet.
Until that exists, a lifted block is something you find on your next commit, not something
that comes looking for you.

`waiting_on:` is separate and required by `status: waiting on owner`: the specific
question the owner has to answer, not a restatement of the title.
-->

